# Backend and governance conventions

- Keep `packages/governance-core` pure: no HTTP framework, environment access,
  filesystem, provider branching, telemetry exporter, or browser dependency.
- There are exactly three provider implementations: deterministic Fake,
  network-free Replay, and server-only Live-Groq. Selection occurs once at the
  runtime composition root.
- Do not create a fourth “mock provider.” Test Live through an injected fake
  HTTP transport.
- Rego is authoritative. Evaluate identity/capability and policy immediately
  before every tool action; missing or malformed output denies.
- Public tools are bounded in-memory operations over synthetic fixtures. No
  shell, arbitrary code, filesystem path, URL fetch, email, database, remote
  DID resolution, or real side effect.
- Live requests use a closed schema containing only `schema_version`, a known
  `scenario_id`, and a finite `variant`. Reject unknown fields.
- Keep Groq credentials in server process environment only. Never serialize,
  log, trace, return, or accept a key from a request.
- Read Groq rate-limit and retry observations from response headers. Treat
  missing/malformed values as unknown; never hardcode provider limits.
- Model output is untrusted data. Validate tool names, arguments, result bounds,
  and state transitions; policy allow/approval is authority.
- Telemetry uses an explicit bounded allowlist and excludes prompts, outputs,
  credentials, tool payloads, CV content, headers, and secrets.
- Public Live remains off until a separate enablement ADR accepts residual cost
  risk and verifies kill switch, budgets, throttling, spend cap, and rollback.
